! Silicon, eight atoms, PBE: the gradient-corrected counterpart of si8-1k.in.
!
! Same cell, same cutoff, same k-point as si8-1k.in -- only the functional
! differs, so the ratio between the two measures what a gradient correction
! costs and nothing else. It is not free: the exchange-correlation term stops
! being a pointwise function of the density and becomes three transforms to
! build grad rho, a pointwise evaluation, and one more transform to take the
! divergence back, all on the dense grid and all once per SCF iteration.
!
! input_dft rather than a PBE dataset on purpose: holding the pseudopotential
! fixed keeps the plane-wave count, the projector count and the starting density
! identical to si8-1k.in, which is what makes the two timings comparable.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=1, celldm(1) =10.20,
    nat=8, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0
    input_dft='pbe'
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.00 0.50 0.50
 Si 0.50 0.00 0.50
 Si 0.50 0.50 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
 Si 0.25 0.75 0.75
 Si 0.75 0.25 0.75
 Si 0.75 0.75 0.25
K_POINTS
  1
   0.250000  0.250000  0.250000   1.00
